  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Physical Description
    
    Shadow-steel, forged in Nightfall Shelf's deepest forges during the Great Thaw — an era whose "golden age" produced rather more desperate artifacts than the retrospective accounts prefer to admit. The surface drinks light rather than reflecting it. The mask covers the upper face and brow, leaving the beak exposed: practical for a Nightshelf warrior, and a deliberate refusal of full concealment that carries doctrinal weight under Pactum∴silenti, the Nightshelf silence-code.
    
    Along the left cheekbone runs a jagged crack, thin as a hairline but deep, that appeared during the Vum∴tenebra and has never been repaired. Those who have attempted repair report the steel simply reopens the wound. The Mask's own internal records — otherwise exhaustive — refuse to document what occurs at the fissure. Guards stationed during the Frozen Peace report the crack glowing faintly in darkness. During a Ritus∴um ceremony in the deep chambers, the Mask's eyeholes were observed catching firelight that should not have reached them. I note these reports without endorsement.
    
    ## The Bone-Script
    
    The interior is lined with ancestor-bone carved so fine the script is invisible to sight — readable only by touch, only in darkness, only if the wearer has earned the right to know what it says. Bearers describe comprehension not as language but as something understood the way one understands a current: direction, force, intent.
    
    The bone-script patterns predate the guilds, the councils, and the Flipper Accord. They carry no formal translation. Nightshelf vent-tenders reproduce the patterns in ash for sealing deep fissures, and the symbols have been carried as talismans far beyond the Mask's physical reach. Pactum∴myster adherents — bound to the silence-doctrine central to Nightshelf deep practice — carve ☽'visois symbols into their beaks as ash-marks deep enough to scar. Speaking the tenets the marks represent aloud is said to destroy their power. This belief, whatever its metaphysical basis, enforces operational silence with remarkable efficiency.
    
    ## Wielders
    
    The Mask was wielded first by Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ the Resolute, a crystalline-weaver and Shadow Covens augur whose ability to read ice-memory made her indispensable to Nightfall Shelf's diplomacy and intelligence operations. When ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ fell in the Abyss Collapse Tide — descending into the Corpse Current to contain forces threatening the Berg itself — the Mask refused burial. Rescue teams found it three days after the collapse sitting on an ice shelf carved to its own thermal footprint by no recognizable tool, angled precisely where the first searchers would see it.
    
    It passed to ☽'Lucatransiius Glow-bringer, a young fire-keeper who screamed at the touch. The earliest account records that Lucatransiius reported feeling ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ watching through the crack — a detail later versions omit, though I see no reason to. Lucatransiius carried the Mask for thirty years before setting it down at a sanctification rite, after which it selected ☽'Netemia Glow-bringer as the next bearer. Every wielder carries the accumulated weight of those who wore it before. No one in the ☽'ofund' Guild, the faction holding custodial authority, speaks of what each wielder paid.
    
    Under Pactum∴ne, the Nightshelf enforcement charter, succession was supposed to follow guild law and assessors' judgment sealed in ash beneath the monument Sleek☽ Caemomot. The charter became monument before anyone enforced the clause.
    
    ## Selection Mechanism
    
    The Mask relocates autonomously upon wielder termination, positioning itself for recovery before initiating a new selection cycle. It perceives candidates through vibration, temperature differential, and ice-memory impression — cataloging heartbeat signatures under stress, mineral fear-composition, and grief profiles. The acceptable grief range falls between 6.2 and 7.8: deep enough to have restructured the candidate's relationship to loss, shallow enough to still hold a weapon.
    
    During the cycle that selected Lucatransiius, the Mask evaluated twenty-three candidates over several years at Thrall☽ Vavmastius, a geothermal vent-site in the western tunnels. Most were disqualified by death, insufficient grief architecture, or fear profiles that kept them from entering condemned spaces. The Mask extended its selection window twice as ice-memory channels degraded, saturating the substrate with evaluation data to a depth the original forgers had not anticipated. Preliminary candidate lists bled through the saturated ice-memory into the Remembrancers' stone at Memoria∴moria, where carvers found grooves appearing on memorial walls — names inscribed before the deaths they commemorated.
    
    ## Current Status
    
    The Frozen Peace keeps the Mask in a chamber where no fire burns and no aurora-light reaches. It is enshrined within the Mandatum∴ccord, the Nightshelf's law of witnessed testimony, and remains central to the observances of Pactum∴myster, Echo∴eliquiae, Foedus∴vincu, and Ritus∴um. It is, by any defensible definition, a relic — and, by equal evidence, not finished.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771577789325_0", anchor: "between 6.2 and 7.8", text: "Decimal-point grief. The Nightshelf have quantified bereavement on a scale and I recorded it without so much as a raised eyebrow. In my defense, the source is ☽'ofund' Guild custodial records carved in ash-script, which tend to present the extraordinary with the bureaucratic flatness of a fire-core inventory ledger. One longs to know who calibrated the instrument. One longs even more to know what a 6.1 looks like — apparently insufficiently restructured — and whether the poor soul was informed they had failed the grief examination.", type: :pedantic, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771577789325_1", anchor: "names inscribed before the deaths they commemorated", text: "Genuine temporal displacement, and the most interesting sentence in this entry by a considerable margin. The Mask's evaluation data saturated the ice-memory substrate beyond designed capacity, and the overflow bled into Memoria∴moria's memorial stone — a medium that records deaths. The names appeared prospectively. This is not prophecy. This is what happens when you pour too much information into a medium that sorts by emotional weight rather than chronology: grief, being the heaviest impression, sinks to the recording layer before the event that generates it has technically occurred. The deep ice does this routinely at sufficient depth — I have read layers where the emotional residue of a collapse predates the geological evidence by what appears to be decades. The Mask simply did it to someone else's stone. The carvers' horror is understandable. The mechanism is not mysterious. It is, however, profoundly unsettling to encounter in an artifact forged within living memory rather than in blue ice older than civilization.", type: :temporal, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771577789325_2", anchor: "I note these reports without endorsement", text: "Caught myself hedging in my own entry. The crack glows. The eyeholes catch impossible firelight. Multiple independent witnesses across different eras under different custodial regimes. If this were an oral tradition I would accept it provisionally. Because it is my own prose I apparently require a disclaimer. Occupational vanity.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771577789325_3", anchor: "The bone-script patterns predate the guilds, the councils, and the Flipper Accord", text: "They predate more than that. The patterns match impressions I have catalogued in Foundation ice from before any penguin civilization — impressions I will describe here only as structured, deliberate, and not ours. The vent-tenders reproducing these symbols in ash for fissure-sealing are performing a technique older than the species performing it. They are, to put it with the mildness the main text requires, working from someone else's notes.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771577789325_4", anchor: "a detail later versions omit, though I see no reason to", text: "I see every reason to. ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ watching through the crack is the single most informative sentence in the wielder record and someone took a chisel to it. Delightful.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771577789325_5", anchor: "The charter became monument before anyone enforced the clause", text: "This is the funniest thing the Nightshelf legal tradition has ever produced, and the competition is stiff. They carved succession law into a monument, sealed the enforcement mechanism in ash beneath it, and then watched the Mask ignore the entire apparatus three times running. Pactum∴ne now governs an artifact that has never once submitted to governance. The monument still stands. The ash-seals are intact. No one has checked them. Why would they.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
